-- title:
--   The Dirichlet idele character admits the modulus (N)
-- statement:
--   Let $N$ be a nonzero natural number and let $\chi$ be a Dirichlet character modulo $N$ with values in $\mathbb{C}$. Attached to $\chi$ is the homomorphism $\chi.\mathrm{dirichletIdeleChar} : (\mathbb{A}_{\mathbb{Q}})^{\times} \to \mathbb{C}^{\times}$, defined as the pointwise inverse of the composite of the monoid homomorphism `unitResidue` $N : (\mathbb{A}_{\mathbb{Q}})^{\times} \to \mathbb{Z}/N$ — which sends an idele unit $x$ to the image under the inverse of the Chinese-remainder isomorphism `ZMod.equivPi` of the tuple of local residues `unitResidues` $N\,x$ — with the unit-valued form of $\chi$; thus $x \mapsto \chi(\mathrm{unitResidue}\,N\,x)^{-1}$. The assertion is [`HeckeCharacter.AdmitsModulus`](def/HeckeCharacter_FiniteOrder.html#L21) for this character over $\mathbb{Q}$ with respect to the ideal of $\mathcal{O}_{\mathbb{Q}}$ generated by $N$: for every unit $u$ of the adele ring $\mathbb{A}_{\mathbb{Q}}$ of $\mathbb{Q}$ whose archimedean component is $1$ and whose finite part satisfies, at every height-one prime $v$ of $\mathcal{O}_{\mathbb{Q}}$, both $\lvert u_v\rvert_v = 1$ and $\lvert u_v - 1\rvert_v \le \exp(-m)$, where $m$ is the multiplicity of $v.\mathrm{asIdeal}$ in the factorisation of the ideal $(N)$, one has $\chi.\mathrm{dirichletIdeleChar}(u) = 1$.
--
--   This says that the idele class character attached to a Dirichlet character modulo $N$ is trivial on the principal congruence subgroup of level $N$ inside the finite ideles (with archimedean component $1$), i.e. that its conductor divides $(N)$; it is the modulus clause in the construction of a finite-order Hecke character from a Dirichlet character. It is used in the comparison of nebentypus data for adelic lifts of modular forms of level $\Gamma_1(N)$ and in the construction of a finite-order Hecke character over $\mathbb{Q}$ with prescribed values on uniformiser ideles and local units.
